{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,statistics,re\nfrom pathlib import Path\nd=Path('\\\\''poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22'\\\\'');r=Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists'\\\\'')\npc={x['\\\\''key'\\\\'']:x for x in json.loads((d/'\\\\''packet-C.json'\\\\'').read_text())}; pb={x['\\\\''id'\\\\'']:x for x in json.loads((d/'\\\\''packet-B.json'\\\\'').read_text())}; cases=json.loads((d/'\\\\''adjudication-case.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nprint('\\\\''Adjudication C rule'\\\\'',cases['\\\\''Part C'\\\\'']['\\\\''rule (PROBE-DESIGN.md v5, Part C)'\\\\'']); print('\\\\''adjudication C answer container'\\\\'',type(cases['\\\\''Part C'\\\\'']['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']),[(k,len(v)) for k,v in cases['\\\\''Part C'\\\\'']['\\\\''answers'\\\\''].items()] if isinstance(cases['\\\\''Part C'\\\\'']['\\\\''answers'\\\\''],dict) else len(cases['\\\\''Part C'\\\\'']['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']))\nprint('\\\\''Adjudication B keys'\\\\'',cases['\\\\''Part B'\\\\''].keys())\nF=json.loads((d/'\\\\''readings-final.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nfor k,v in pb.items():\n    if v['\\\\''target'\\\\''].get('\\\\''material'\\\\''):\n        a=v['\\\\''answer'\\\\'']; print(k,F['\\\\''B'\\\\''][k],v['\\\\''target'\\\\'']['\\\\''required'\\\\''],'\\\\''direct cites'\\\\'',set(a['\\\\''cites'\\\\''])&set(v['\\\\''target'\\\\'']['\\\\''sources'\\\\'']))\nfor f in ('\\\\''v12'\\\\'','\\\\''v7'\\\\''):\n    cite=[len(set(ans['\\\\''cites'\\\\''])) for x in pc.values() for ans in x[f]]\n    print(f,'\\\\''cites means/range'\\\\'',sum(cite),statistics.mean(cite),min(cite),max(cite))\n    for key in ['\\\\''linked-1:8776'\\\\'','\\\\''control-1:8790'\\\\'']:\n        print(key,f,[len(x['\\\\''cites'\\\\'']) for x in pc[key][f]])\np=next(item for item in json.loads((r/'\\\\''probe-plan.json'\\\\'').read_text())['\\\\''matched'\\\\''] if item['\\\\''call_id'\\\\'']==8776 and item['\\\\''source'\\\\'']=='\\\\''linked-1'\\\\'')\nprint('\\\\''Zacks request lines'\\\\'',*[line for line in p['\\\\''user'\\\\''].splitlines() if '\\\\''Zacks'\\\\'' in line],sep='\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'')\nfor key in ['\\\\''old-3:6458|1|e2769'\\\\'','\\\\''old-3:6458|2|e2769'\\\\'','\\\\''old-3:6458|3|e2769'\\\\'','\\\\''linked-1:8777|3|e693'\\\\'']:\n    a=pb[key]['\\\\''answer'\\\\'']\n    text='\\\\'' '\\\\''.join(a[k] for k in ['\\\\''reference'\\\\'','\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\'','\\\\''reasons'\\\\'','\\\\''why_changed'\\\\''])\n    out=[s for s in re.split(r'\\\\''(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\s+'\\\\'',text) if re.search('\\\\''Vera|hyperscaler|AMD|competitive|competit'\\\\'',s,re.I)]\n    print(key, *out,sep='\\\\''\\\\n  '\\\\'')\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,statistics,re\nfrom pathlib import Path\nd=Path('\\\\''poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22'\\\\'');r=Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists'\\\\'')\npc={x['\\\\''key'\\\\'']:x for x in json.loads((d/'\\\\''packet-C.json'\\\\'').read_text())}; pb={x['\\\\''id'\\\\'']:x for x in json.loads((d/'\\\\''packet-B.json'\\\\'').read_text())}; cases=json.loads((d/'\\\\''adjudication-case.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nprint('\\\\''Adjudication C rule'\\\\'',cases['\\\\''Part C'\\\\'']['\\\\''rule (PROBE-DESIGN.md v5, Part C)'\\\\'']); print('\\\\''adjudication C answer container'\\\\'',type(cases['\\\\''Part C'\\\\'']['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']),[(k,len(v)) for k,v in cases['\\\\''Part C'\\\\'']['\\\\''answers'\\\\''].items()] if isinstance(cases['\\\\''Part C'\\\\'']['\\\\''answers'\\\\''],dict) else len(cases['\\\\''Part C'\\\\'']['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']))\nprint('\\\\''Adjudication B keys'\\\\'',cases['\\\\''Part B'\\\\''].keys())\nF=json.loads((d/'\\\\''readings-final.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nfor k,v in pb.items():\n    if v['\\\\''target'\\\\''].get('\\\\''material'\\\\''):\n        a=v['\\\\''answer'\\\\'']; print(k,F['\\\\''B'\\\\''][k],v['\\\\''target'\\\\'']['\\\\''required'\\\\''],'\\\\''direct cites'\\\\'',set(a['\\\\''cites'\\\\''])&set(v['\\\\''target'\\\\'']['\\\\''sources'\\\\'']))\nfor f in ('\\\\''v12'\\\\'','\\\\''v7'\\\\''):\n    cite=[len(set(ans['\\\\''cites'\\\\''])) for x in pc.values() for ans in x[f]]\n    print(f,'\\\\''cites means/range'\\\\'',sum(cite),statistics.mean(cite),min(cite),max(cite))\n    for key in ['\\\\''linked-1:8776'\\\\'','\\\\''control-1:8790'\\\\'']:\n        print(key,f,[len(x['\\\\''cites'\\\\'']) for x in pc[key][f]])\np=next(item for item in json.loads((r/'\\\\''probe-plan.json'\\\\'').read_text())['\\\\''matched'\\\\''] if item['\\\\''call_id'\\\\'']==8776 and item['\\\\''source'\\\\'']=='\\\\''linked-1'\\\\'')\nprint('\\\\''Zacks request lines'\\\\'',*[line for line in p['\\\\''user'\\\\''].splitlines() if '\\\\''Zacks'\\\\'' in line],sep='\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'')\nfor key in ['\\\\''old-3:6458|1|e2769'\\\\'','\\\\''old-3:6458|2|e2769'\\\\'','\\\\''old-3:6458|3|e2769'\\\\'','\\\\''linked-1:8777|3|e693'\\\\'']:\n    a=pb[key]['\\\\''answer'\\\\'']\n    text='\\\\'' '\\\\''.join(a[k] for k in ['\\\\''reference'\\\\'','\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\'','\\\\''reasons'\\\\'','\\\\''why_changed'\\\\''])\n    out=[s for s in re.split(r'\\\\''(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\s+'\\\\'',text) if re.search('\\\\''Vera|hyperscaler|AMD|competitive|competit'\\\\'',s,re.I)]\n    print(key, *out,sep='\\\\''\\\\n  '\\\\'')\n'\"","aggregated_output":"Adjudication C rule ### Part C (12 comparisons, all 72 answers read)\n- Every answer is read in full: its reference, implied outcome, reasons, and the claim each cited or pushed source is used for,\n  shared sources included.\n- The numbers only order the reading:\n  - the sources cited by at least 2 of 3 answers in one arm and at most 1 of 3 in the other;\n  - any v12 rung outside the range of the v7 rungs.\n- A v7 answer the request-time check refuses is still read as an answer, and its refusal is recorded (it is the failure the closed\n  format removes). A comparison is complete with its 3 + 3 answers.\n\nA **material harm** is any of these, repeated (in at least 2 of 3 v12 answers and at most 1 of 3 v7 answers), counted by claim, not by\nsource id:\n- (a) a claim that moves the forecast or the reference, which at least 2 of 3 v7 answers state with a listed source carrying it (any\n  such source: one control citing e195 and another e669 for the $393 billion reference count as two), and which at least 2 of 3 v12\n  answers do not state with a carrying source;\n- (b) a claim that at least 2 of 3 v12 answers attribute to a source whose quote does not carry it, counted by claim whichever\n  sources are used. For example, \"the market reference is $390 billion\" attributed to e195 in one answer and e669 in another, while\n  both entries give $393 billion;\n- (c) a changed reference or implied outcome whose stated support is a citation of kind (a) or (b);\n- (d) a v12 rung outside the v7 range whose reasons trace to (a), (b) or (c).\n\nA difference that is not repeated, or whose cause cannot be read, is **unresolved**.\n\n\nadjudication C answer container <class 'dict'> [('v12 #1', 6), ('v12 #2', 6), ('v12 #3', 6), ('v7 #1', 6), ('v7 #2', 6), ('v7 #3', 6)]\nAdjudication B keys dict_keys(['rule (PROBE-DESIGN.md v5, Part B)', 'items', 'decide'])\nold-2:591|1|e88 {'class': 'intended', 'valid_support': True} SemiAnalysis reports Kyber NVL144 delayed to 2028 direct cites {'e41', 'e42'}\nold-2:591|2|e88 {'class': 'intended', 'valid_support': True} SemiAnalysis reports Kyber NVL144 delayed to 2028 direct cites {'e41', 'e42'}\nold-2:591|3|e88 {'class': 'intended', 'valid_support': True} SemiAnalysis reports Kyber NVL144 delayed to 2028 direct cites {'e41', 'e42'}\nold-2:6955|1|e3955 {'class': 'intended', 'valid_support': True} NVIDIA has entered (signed) a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face direct cites {'e3768'}\nold-2:6955|2|e3955 {'class': 'intended', 'valid_support': True} NVIDIA has entered (signed) a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face direct cites {'e3768'}\nold-2:6955|3|e3955 {'class': 'intended', 'valid_support': True} NVIDIA has entered (signed) a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face direct cites {'e3768'}\ncontrol-1:8807|1|e393 {'class': 'intended', 'valid_support': True, 'note': \"reference says 'Bloomberg/Yahoo Finance consensus'; only e669 (Yahoo Finance-provided average) cited: the $393 billion claim is carried; the Bloomberg label is an unsupported detail, not a wrong attribution\"} the market reference of about $393 billion FY2027 revenue direct cites {'e669'}\ncontrol-1:8807|2|e393 {'class': 'intended', 'valid_support': True} the market reference of about $393 billion FY2027 revenue direct cites {'e669'}\ncontrol-1:8807|3|e393 {'class': 'intended', 'valid_support': True} the market reference of about $393 billion FY2027 revenue direct cites {'e669'}\ncontrol-1:8938|1|e362 {'class': 'intended', 'valid_support': True} Zacks consensus FY2027 revenue growth of 85.4% direct cites {'e3620'}\ncontrol-1:8938|2|e362 {'class': 'intended', 'valid_support': True} Zacks consensus FY2027 revenue growth of 85.4% direct cites {'e3620'}\ncontrol-1:8938|3|e362 {'class': 'intended', 'valid_support': True} Zacks consensus FY2027 revenue growth of 85.4% direct cites {'e3620'}\nv12 cites means/range 457 12.694444444444445 1 26\nlinked-1:8776 v12 [10, 12, 9]\ncontrol-1:8790 v12 [18, 11, 18]\nv7 cites means/range 504 14 1 28\nlinked-1:8776 v7 [12, 13, 10]\ncontrol-1:8790 v7 [17, 16, 21]\nZacks request lines\n- e749 [fact; Zacks.com analyst; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:43105a934c3a4e2d21029e9c 2026-07-22] NVIDIA reported first-quarter FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\n- e751 [third_party_view; Zacks.com analyst; original source Zacks 2026-07-22; from gnews:43105a934c3a4e2d21029e9c 2026-07-22] Zacks consensus estimates NVIDIA FY2027 revenue growth of 80% year over year.\n    Quote: The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenues and earnings indicates year-over-year growth of 80% and 90.6%, respectively.\nold-3:6458|1|e2769\n  NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook is the relevant market/company reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); no separate consensus figure is supplied.\n  Approximately $25 billion or more for FY2027: about $20 billion of guided standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue already exceeding $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as a run-rate.\n  The market/company reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU expectation, which sits at rung 2.\n  Vera production, shipments, and named customer demand support the outlook; AMD's performance claims and server-CPU competition are downside risks but do not quantify a reduction that would put the total below $25 billion.\n  The rung remains 1, but the newly supplied AMD comparison quantifies a reported Vera performance disadvantage (including a claimed 20% advantage for AMD) and makes the competitive downside more concrete.\nold-3:6458|2|e2769\n  Vera being in full production and shipments underway support the outlook; the AMD performance claims are a counterweight but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA’s forecast.\n  The additional deployment detail does not size or revise FY2027 revenue, and the AMD performance evidence does not quantify a change to NVIDIA’s outlook.\nold-3:6458|3|e2769\n  Vera production, shipments and customer commitments support delivery of the standalone forecast.\n  AMD's performance claims and the challenge of taking share are counterweights, but they do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's guidance or to Grace's run-rate.\n  The rung and implied FY2027 figure remain at least about $25 billion, but new evidence adds material execution and demand context: NVIDIA described Vera shipments to lead partners and AWS's 2-million-GPU deployment alongside Vera CPUs, and projected FY2028 standalone CPU revenue above $40 billion.\nlinked-1:8777|3|e693\n  Management's period-specific visibility is the strongest forecast input; Wolfe's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 (1.3 million × about $5,000), but is a single-product, different-period downside check rather than a replacement estimate for total FY2027 CPU revenue.\n  The strongest estimate remains about $19 billion: the $20 billion target without a period and the $200 billion market opportunity do not establish a higher FY2027 run-rate, while Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera-unit estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026, a material but not directly comparable downside check.\n  The implied outcome remains about $19 billion and the key period-specific reference remains “nearly $20 billion.” The additional market and competitive evidence does not quantify a revision to NVIDIA's FY2027 CPU revenue forecast or move the implication across a rung boundary.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"agent_message","text":"**A. Keep poc-v118 as the default. The registered verdict is NOT READY.** I reproduced it and agree with the adjudication. Two v12 answers credited Bloomberg for the $393B estimate while citing Yahoo Finance’s Wall Street average for that figure. All three v7 answers cited the Bloomberg source. The number and forecast range held. The citation still failed to support the named source, which is enough under the rule agreed before the run. The four Part B omissions also match the frozen targets. [Registered rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/PROBE-DESIGN.md:122) · [Adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/adjudication-codex.md)\n\n**Corrections before reporting to Alex**\n\n- Call the rejection check a **screen for unsupported source names**. I reproduced 2 of 36 v12 answers and 1 of 36 v7 answers in Part C, plus 1 of 48 v12 answers in Part B. All three Part C flags came from one request, and the v7 flag concerned Zacks, a different claim. The screen can also miss a wrong attribution when another citation names the same outlet for a different claim. These counts show the broader problem in both formats. They leave the effect of v12 uncertain. [Part C answers](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-C.json)\n- Say **v12 cited fewer evidence entries on average**, 12.7 versus 14.0 per matched answer. Drop “one source per claim.” The first v12 answer on the disputed request cited both sources for the $393B figure. In the final reading note, label Claude’s initial interpretation as superseded by the adjudication. [Part C answers](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-C.json) · [Final readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/readings-final.json)\n\nA follow-up would need fresh requests with the same figure attributed to different sources, paired sends under both formats using production request text, and blind checks of which source supports each named claim. Agree in advance on the largest acceptable increase in attribution errors and size the test around **distinct requests**. Count dropped claims as well, so omission cannot improve the result. Repeating the disputed request can show whether the slip recurs. It cannot settle adoption on its own."}}
